-- title:
--   Denominators over ℤ_{(ℓ)}[j] in the modular function field
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $\ell$ a prime, and write $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$ for the subring [`GaloisRep.ratLocalizedAt`](def/GaloisRep_Flat.html#L8) $\ell$ of $\mathbb{Q}$ consisting of those rationals whose denominator is coprime to $\ell$. Let $F =$ `modularFunctionFieldFull N` be the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$ (Laurent series over $\mathbb{Q}$) generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by the set of all $q$-expansions $j(q^{d})$, i.e. by the images `qExpand ℚ d jq` of the $j$-expansion `jq` under substitution $q \mapsto q^{d}$, as $d$ runs over the nonzero divisors of $N$. Let $x \in F$ be arbitrary. The assertion is that there exists an element $g$ of the $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-subalgebra of $F$ generated by the single element `jFull N`, that is by $j$ itself, such that $g \neq 0$ and the product $g \cdot x$ lies in the $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}$-subalgebra of $F$ generated by the two elements $j$ and `jqN N` $= j(q^{N})$, the latter viewed in $F$ via its membership in that field.
--
--   This is the statement that every element of the modular function field of level $N$ admits a nonzero denominator in $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}[j]$ clearing it into $\mathbb{Z}_{(\ell)}[j, j(q^{N})]$; it rests on the generation of the function field by $j$ and $j(q^{N})$ together with the integrality of $j(q^{N})$ over $\mathbb{Z}[j]$. It is used in the construction and analysis of the Igusa scheme at level $N$ and prime $\ell$, in particular in identifying the chart algebras and their integral closures and in the computation of stalks at the generic point.
